1
00:00:01,360 --> 00:00:04,080
April the first — the day before her first

2
00:00:04,160 --> 00:00:05,200
cup of chai.

3
00:00:05,520 --> 00:00:06,640
The sun isn't up yet.

4
00:00:07,040 --> 00:00:10,040
Meera is holding fifty thousand rupees —

5
00:00:10,080 --> 00:00:11,600
years of her savings.

6
00:00:12,849 --> 00:00:15,009
And just like that, it's in the galla.

7
00:00:15,489 --> 00:00:17,409
So — whose money is it now?

8
00:00:17,809 --> 00:00:18,369
Meera's?

9
00:00:19,209 --> 00:00:20,009
Or the stall's?

10
00:00:20,462 --> 00:00:22,889
The answer is… a bit of both.

11
00:00:28,753 --> 00:00:30,753
First, the answers from last time.

12
00:00:31,313 --> 00:00:31,633
One.

13
00:00:32,113 --> 00:00:34,113
Meera pays her personal phone bill from

14
00:00:34,193 --> 00:00:35,553
her own bank account.

15
00:00:35,793 --> 00:00:37,633
Does it go in the stall's books?

16
00:00:38,393 --> 00:00:38,953
No.

17
00:00:39,193 --> 00:00:40,873
Her money, her bill.

18
00:00:40,913 --> 00:00:42,953
The stall is its own person.

19
00:00:43,411 --> 00:00:43,811
Two.

20
00:00:44,211 --> 00:00:46,691
A customer asks the price of chai, then

21
00:00:46,771 --> 00:00:47,611
walks away.

22
00:00:47,891 --> 00:00:49,051
Not a transaction.

23
00:00:49,251 --> 00:00:50,691
Nothing changed hands.

24
00:00:51,147 --> 00:00:51,787
Three.

25
00:00:52,027 --> 00:00:53,707
Which report is the photo?

26
00:00:54,547 --> 00:00:55,627
The Balance Sheet.

27
00:00:55,907 --> 00:00:58,867
And today, we start filling in that photo.

28
00:00:59,926 --> 00:01:01,846
Let's start with what the stall has.

29
00:01:02,326 --> 00:01:04,406
Right now, that's fifty thousand rupees

30
00:01:04,446 --> 00:01:05,046
in the galla.

31
00:01:05,446 --> 00:01:06,046
Cash.

32
00:01:06,646 --> 00:01:07,846
And cash is useful.

33
00:01:08,246 --> 00:01:10,486
Meera can buy milk with it, pay rent with

34
00:01:10,606 --> 00:01:11,926
it, buy a cart with it.

35
00:01:12,406 --> 00:01:14,486
Anything a business has that is useful to

36
00:01:14,566 --> 00:01:16,086
it is called an asset.

37
00:01:17,366 --> 00:01:18,846
Cash is an asset.

38
00:01:19,126 --> 00:01:21,286
Soon the cart will be an asset too, and

39
00:01:21,366 --> 00:01:22,526
so will the tea leaves.

40
00:01:23,226 --> 00:01:26,346
From now on, every asset gets its own jar.

41
00:01:26,586 --> 00:01:29,506
Each jar is what accountants call an account.

42
00:01:30,306 --> 00:01:32,786
And the cash jar — the cash account?

43
00:01:33,626 --> 00:01:34,666
That's the galla.

44
00:01:35,717 --> 00:01:38,637
But accountants always ask one more question.

45
00:01:38,917 --> 00:01:40,517
Where did it come from?

46
00:01:41,277 --> 00:01:43,197
Every asset has a source.

47
00:01:43,277 --> 00:01:44,637
So let's tag the money.

48
00:01:45,398 --> 00:01:47,878
This fifty thousand came from Meera.

49
00:01:47,958 --> 00:01:50,278
The stall holds the money, but Meera has

50
00:01:50,358 --> 00:01:51,478
a claim on it.

51
00:01:51,558 --> 00:01:53,878
If the stall ever closed, this share would

52
00:01:53,958 --> 00:01:55,158
go back to her.

53
00:01:55,838 --> 00:01:57,758
The owner's share of a business is called

54
00:01:57,918 --> 00:01:58,798
equity.

55
00:01:59,638 --> 00:02:01,918
And the money an owner puts in has its own

56
00:02:02,038 --> 00:02:03,598
name — capital.

57
00:02:03,678 --> 00:02:06,398
Meera's capital: fifty thousand rupees.

58
00:02:07,463 --> 00:02:10,183
Later that morning, someone else turns up.

59
00:02:10,423 --> 00:02:11,303
Ravi Mama.

60
00:02:11,543 --> 00:02:12,183
Umbrella.

61
00:02:12,423 --> 00:02:13,343
Moustache.

62
00:02:13,543 --> 00:02:14,903
Very proud of his niece.

63
00:02:15,383 --> 00:02:17,810
He hands Meera thirty thousand rupees —

64
00:02:17,836 --> 00:02:18,503
a loan.

65
00:02:18,743 --> 00:02:20,503
She'll pay it back once the stall is doing

66
00:02:20,583 --> 00:02:21,143
well.

67
00:02:21,899 --> 00:02:24,939
The galla now holds eighty thousand rupees.

68
00:02:25,019 --> 00:02:27,379
So what tag goes on this new money?

69
00:02:28,259 --> 00:02:29,579
Not Meera's face.

70
00:02:29,779 --> 00:02:31,939
It came from Ravi Mama, and the stall has

71
00:02:31,979 --> 00:02:32,939
to give it back.

72
00:02:33,819 --> 00:02:36,059
Money a business owes to someone else is

73
00:02:36,219 --> 00:02:37,579
called a liability.

74
00:02:38,419 --> 00:02:40,899
Ravi Mama's loan is a liability.

75
00:02:41,962 --> 00:02:44,682
Now, here's where a lot of people slip.

76
00:02:44,762 --> 00:02:47,322
The galla went from fifty thousand to eighty

77
00:02:47,402 --> 00:02:48,202
thousand.

78
00:02:48,282 --> 00:02:51,162
So Meera is thirty thousand rupees richer!

79
00:02:52,291 --> 00:02:52,891
Nope.

80
00:02:53,171 --> 00:02:56,051
Yes, the cash went up by thirty thousand.

81
00:02:56,131 --> 00:02:58,051
But what the stall owes went up by thirty

82
00:02:58,131 --> 00:02:59,171
thousand too.

83
00:02:59,851 --> 00:03:00,971
Meera's own share?

84
00:03:01,651 --> 00:03:03,011
Still fifty thousand.

85
00:03:03,091 --> 00:03:04,531
Not one rupee more.

86
00:03:05,411 --> 00:03:07,571
And a liability isn't a bad thing.

87
00:03:07,651 --> 00:03:09,811
It's someone else's money, working for your

88
00:03:10,051 --> 00:03:10,691
business.

89
00:03:10,771 --> 00:03:13,331
You just have to remember it isn't yours.

90
00:03:14,378 --> 00:03:16,378
So let's look at the whole picture.

91
00:03:16,458 --> 00:03:18,778
The stall has eighty thousand rupees.

92
00:03:18,858 --> 00:03:20,778
Thirty thousand of it is claimed by Ravi

93
00:03:20,818 --> 00:03:21,498
Mama.

94
00:03:21,538 --> 00:03:23,898
Fifty thousand is claimed by Meera.

95
00:03:23,978 --> 00:03:26,858
Every rupee in the business belongs to someone.

96
00:03:27,738 --> 00:03:30,458
In one sentence: what you have equals what

97
00:03:30,538 --> 00:03:32,938
you owe, plus what's yours.

98
00:03:33,618 --> 00:03:34,978
Remember that sentence.

99
00:03:35,058 --> 00:03:37,138
Next lesson, it gets a scale.

100
00:03:38,394 --> 00:03:40,834
Let's sort some things into three boxes.

101
00:03:41,034 --> 00:03:43,314
What the stall has — assets.

102
00:03:43,514 --> 00:03:45,594
What it owes — liabilities.

103
00:03:45,914 --> 00:03:48,394
And what belongs to the owner — equity.

104
00:03:48,634 --> 00:03:49,834
Khata goes first.

105
00:03:49,994 --> 00:03:51,354
The cash in the galla.

106
00:03:53,283 --> 00:03:53,523
Asset!

107
00:03:53,683 --> 00:03:56,563
The stall has it, and it's useful.

108
00:03:57,113 --> 00:03:57,993
Meera's turn.

109
00:03:58,473 --> 00:03:59,913
Ravi Mama's loan.

110
00:04:01,482 --> 00:04:02,242
Careful.

111
00:04:02,442 --> 00:04:04,762
The cash he gave is an asset.

112
00:04:04,802 --> 00:04:07,402
But the loan is what the stall owes.

113
00:04:07,482 --> 00:04:08,442
Liability.

114
00:04:09,164 --> 00:04:09,884
Your turn.

115
00:04:10,124 --> 00:04:12,844
A stack of glasses Meera buys tomorrow.

116
00:04:16,070 --> 00:04:16,950
Asset.

117
00:04:16,990 --> 00:04:19,590
The stall will have them, and use them every

118
00:04:19,670 --> 00:04:20,150
day.

119
00:04:20,719 --> 00:04:21,679
And one more.

120
00:04:21,919 --> 00:04:24,799
April's rent — she owes it, but hasn't paid

121
00:04:24,879 --> 00:04:25,519
it yet.

122
00:04:28,673 --> 00:04:29,553
Liability.

123
00:04:30,033 --> 00:04:32,353
That money is owed to the landlord.

124
00:04:33,604 --> 00:04:34,004
So.

125
00:04:34,404 --> 00:04:36,804
Assets are what the business has.

126
00:04:36,884 --> 00:04:39,604
Liabilities are what it owes to others.

127
00:04:39,684 --> 00:04:42,244
Equity is what belongs to the owner.

128
00:04:42,324 --> 00:04:44,404
And every rupee in the business belongs

129
00:04:44,484 --> 00:04:45,284
to someone.

130
00:04:46,435 --> 00:04:47,075
Your turn.

131
00:04:47,235 --> 00:04:48,235
Three quick ones.

132
00:04:48,835 --> 00:04:49,315
One.

133
00:04:49,555 --> 00:04:51,715
Gopal Dairy delivers eight thousand rupees

134
00:04:51,795 --> 00:04:54,075
of milk, and Meera will pay later.

135
00:04:54,355 --> 00:04:56,595
What does the stall now have, and what does

136
00:04:56,675 --> 00:04:57,635
it owe?

137
00:04:59,515 --> 00:05:00,155
Two.

138
00:05:00,235 --> 00:05:02,395
Is Meera's personal scooter an asset of

139
00:05:02,475 --> 00:05:03,435
the stall?

140
00:05:05,315 --> 00:05:05,955
Three.

141
00:05:06,035 --> 00:05:08,275
The stall has eighty thousand rupees, and

142
00:05:08,355 --> 00:05:10,435
owes Ravi Mama thirty thousand.

143
00:05:10,515 --> 00:05:11,955
What's Meera's share?

144
00:05:13,995 --> 00:05:15,995
Answers at the start of the next lesson.

145
00:05:17,413 --> 00:05:20,293
Next time, Meera spends thirty-six thousand

146
00:05:20,373 --> 00:05:23,013
rupees on a cart, a stove and a stack of

147
00:05:23,093 --> 00:05:24,053
glasses.

148
00:05:24,213 --> 00:05:26,693
So — did she just lose thirty-six thousand

149
00:05:26,773 --> 00:05:27,493
rupees?
